      "text": "To determine if two lines are perpendicular, their slopes must be negative reciprocals of each other.\nFor the first line, $y = 2x+13$, the slope is $m_1 = 2$.\nFor the second line, $6y+ax = 6$, we can rewrite it as $6y = ax-6$ and divide by 6 to get $y = \\frac {a}{6} x - 1$.\nSo the slope of the second line is $m_2 = \\frac{a}{6}$.\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, $m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1$.\nSo, $2 \\cdot \\frac{a}{6} = -1$ which gives $a = -3$.\n\nTo determine if two lines are perpendicular, we need to look at their slopes.\nThe first equation is $y = 2x+13$. This can be rewritten as $2x - y = -13$ so its slope is $2$.\nThe second equation is $6y + ax = 6$. Rewriting it gives $6y = -ax + 6$ which can be rearranged as $y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1$. So the slope of this line is $-\\frac{a}{6}$.\nIf two lines are perpendicular, then one slope is the negative reciprocal of the other.\nThus, we have the equation $2 \\cdot \\left(- \\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1$ and solving for $a$, we find $a= \\boxed{-3}$.\nThe answer is: -3",
