      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) that makes the given lines perpendicular, we need to analyze their slopes. Given:\n\n1. \\( y = 2x + 13 \\)\n2. \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\)\n\nFirst, we'll find the slope of each line by manipulating the equations into slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) represents the slope.\n\n### Step 1: Convert the first equation into slope-intercept form\n\nGiven slope-intercept form is:\n\\[ y = mx + b \\]\n\nThe first line is:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13 \\]\n\nComparing with the standard form, we see that:\n\\[ m_1 = 2 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Convert the second equation into slope-intercept form\n\nGiven slope-intercept form is:\n\\[ y = mx + b \\]\n\nRewriting the second line:\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6 \\]\n\nSubtract \\( ax \\) from both sides:\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6 \\]\n\nDivide through by 6:\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the slope is \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\n### Step 3: Determine the condition for perpendicular lines\n\nFor two lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\n\nSubstitute the slopes we found:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1 \\]\n\n### Step 4: Solve for \\( a \\)\n\nSolve the equation for \\( a \\):\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1 \\]\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1 \\]\n\\[ a = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
